    {
      "body": "thank you for contributing this. the #8840 assumes OOM not an issue and the chunked buffer idea is dropped. My bad of missing updating the PR description accordingly.",
      "created_at": "2026-11-04T01:27:01Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/deepspeedai/DeepSpeed/pull/8986#issuecomment-6620007318",
      "id": 6620007318,
      "updated_at": "2026-11-04T01:27:15Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "pengdurice"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "LGTM, @sfc-gh-truwase , would you mind taking a look as well? ;--) ",
      "created_at": "2026-11-04T03:22:02Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/deepspeedai/DeepSpeed/pull/8986#issuecomment-6621650121",
      "id": 6621650121,
      "updated_at": "2026-11-04T03:22:02Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "pengdurice"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "@xylian86 my concern with @pengdurice's original code is that the OOM risk is minimal to justify the extra complexity of a chunked approach. Did you encounter an OOM issue?",
      "created_at": "2026-11-04T04:11:16Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/deepspeedai/DeepSpeed/pull/8986#issuecomment-6622320014",
      "id": 6622320014,
      "updated_at": "2026-11-04T04:11:16Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "sfc-gh-truwase"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "@sfc-gh-truwase No, not in a real run.\r\n- You're right that it does not raise the allocated peak: forward/backward sets it.\r\n- The temporary is a whole sub-group in float32: up to 3.7 GiB per rank at the default sub_group_size.\r\n- But the allocator keeps it cached, so near the limit the forward/backward after step() OOMs from fragmentation.\r\n\r\nSo this PR can still let a slightly larger model fit, but I'm also fine with closing it.",
      "created_at": "2026-11-04T05:07:23Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/deepspeedai/DeepSpeed/pull/8986#issuecomment-6623091262",
      "id": 6623091262,
      "updated_at": "2026-11-04T05:07:23Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "xylian86"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "@xylian86 thanks for confirming the details. I think we can close for now as there are multiple efforts to improve offloading performance :). I am very delighted by the renewed focus on offloading. ",
      "created_at": "2026-11-04T11:29:17Z",
