      "body": "Read at `8e6295a1`. This looks right to me: the pieces are views of the pinned buffer, so `non_blocking` still applies, and since they run in order on one stream, the allocator can reuse each piece's temporary for the next one.\n\nYour test skips on the CPU accelerator, so I checked the chunking directly. I called `_copy_pinned_master_to_device` with `PINNED_MASTER_COPY_NUMEL` patched to 1000, a no-op event (the CPU accelerator has none) and an fp32 source of 0, 1, 999, 1000, 2000 and 2500 elements: the bf16 destination equals `src.to(torch.bfloat16)` every time, tails and the empty case included. I can't check the device-memory numbers here. A small test along those lines, with the constant patched down, would let the CPU job cover the tail logic too.",
